PRISCA_THEOLOGIA_EXPANDED = """<p><i>Prisca Theologia</i> (Latin: "ancient theology") is an Actor Term denoting the Renaissance and early modern conviction that a unified, primordial divine wisdom had been transmitted through a succession of ancient pre-Christian sages — beginning with Zoroaster and Hermes Trismegistus, passing through the Orphic tradition, Pythagoras, Plato, and the Hebrew prophets — and that this original, pure theology could be recovered and integrated with Christian revelation. The doctrine was given its canonical formulation by Marsilio Ficino in the 1460s and became the governing intellectual framework for Florentine Neoplatonism and, more broadly, for Renaissance magical philosophy. Crucially, <i>Prisca Theologia</i> served as an ideological legitimation for studying and practicing natural magic, alchemy, and other esoteric disciplines: if the greatest ancient sages had engaged with these arts, then Renaissance scholars who pursued them were not corrupting truth but recovering it. The belief that pagan wisdom and Christian truth could be harmonized through <i>Prisca Theologia</i> shaped intellectual and religious discourse for over a century.</p>

<h2>Historical Usage</h2>

<p>The intellectual roots of <i>Prisca Theologia</i> lie in medieval and early Renaissance scholarship on Plato and Neoplatonism. By the fourteenth century, Renaissance humanists had begun to recover manuscripts of Plato's dialogues and Neoplatonic commentaries, which had been largely unavailable in the Latin West. The discovery of what was believed to be Hermes Trismegistus's doctrines — the <i>Corpus Hermeticum</i> texts translated by Ficino in the 1460s — seemed to confirm that a continuous lineage of divine wisdom existed. Ficino's presentation of <i>Prisca Theologia</i> in his <i>Platonic Theology</i> (completed in 1469) was methodically constructed: God, in his infinite wisdom, had given the same essential truths to multiple ancient sources, establishing a <i>theologia perennis</i> (perennial theology) that transcended particular cultures while remaining fundamentally coherent.</p>

<p>Ficino explicitly positioned Hermes Trismegistus as the oldest and most authoritative witness to this ancient wisdom, predating even Zoroaster and Plato. He believed Hermes was a historical figure — an Egyptian priest or sage who had lived in antiquity and whose teachings had percolated through the Greek philosophical tradition. The <i>Corpus Hermeticum</i>, which Ficino had translated under the assumption that it represented genuinely ancient wisdom (an assumption undermined only after Isaac Casaubon's 1614 dating), served as the textual anchor for the entire apparatus of <i>Prisca Theologia</i>. If one could trust the Hermetica, one could trust the doctrine of perennial wisdom. The theological implications were enormous: the tools of Hermetic magic — the manipulation of celestial and planetary forces, the crafting of talismans, the practice of sacred ceremony — were not demonic impostures but legitimate expressions of the divine order that the ancient sages had understood.</p>

<p>The doctrine achieved its widest diffusion in the late sixteenth and seventeenth centuries. Renaissance thinkers like Pico della Mirandola, in his <i>Oration on the Dignity of Man</i> (1486), invoked <i>Prisca Theologia</i> to argue that all traditions — Jewish Kabbalah, Islamic philosophy, Christian theology, Hermetic magic — were expressions of the same underlying truth. By the early seventeenth century, the doctrine permeated educational and religious circles throughout Europe. The alchemist and physician Paracelsus (though he wrote before the systematic articulation of <i>Prisca Theologia</i> as a coherent program) invoked the concept of ancient wisdom to legitimize his revolutionary approach to medicine and chemistry. Theurgists and magical philosophers like Henry Cornelius Agrippa (1486–1535), in his <i>Three Books of Occult Philosophy</i> (1531), systematically drew on the notion that Hermes, Plato, Pythagoras, and the Kabbalah all taught the same doctrines of correspondence, celestial magic, and the divine order of nature.</p>

<h2>Scholarly Significance</h2>

<p>The critical turning point in the scholarly understanding of <i>Prisca Theologia</i> came with Isaac Casaubon's 1614 philological analysis, which demonstrated that the <i>Corpus Hermeticum</i> was not ancient Egyptian wisdom but a Greco-Roman pseudepigraphical text dating to roughly the second century CE. This single scholarly intervention effectively demolished the historical foundation of <i>Prisca Theologia</i> as a doctrine of continuous ancient wisdom. However, rather than disappearing, <i>Prisca Theologia</i> underwent a profound transformation in how it was understood and valued.</p>

<p>Modern scholarship, particularly the work of D. P. Walker in <i>The Ancient Theology: Studies in Christian Platonism from the 15th to the 18th Centuries</i> (1972) and Wouter Hanegraaff in <i>Hermetic Spirituality and the Historical Imagination</i> (2022), has reframed <i>Prisca Theologia</i> not as a failed historical thesis but as a projection — a powerful intellectual and spiritual strategy through which Renaissance thinkers sought to reconcile pagan wisdom with Christian truth, and to legitimize the study of natural magic and esotericism. Rather than "ancient theology," <i>Prisca Theologia</i> can be understood as the Renaissance reinvention of antiquity in service of a new intellectual and spiritual agenda. Frances Yates, in <i>Giordano Bruno and the Hermetic Tradition</i> (1964), argued that <i>Prisca Theologia</i> and the belief in Hermetic wisdom were central to the development of Renaissance magic and its influence on the Scientific Revolution. However, this "Yates Paradigm" has itself been substantially revised by subsequent scholarship, which argues that Yates overstated both the intellectual coherence of Hermeticism and its direct causal influence on early modern science.</p>

<p>Contemporary scholarship emphasizes the strategic function of <i>Prisca Theologia</i>: it allowed Renaissance thinkers to study astrology, alchemy, and magic without direct condemnation from ecclesiastical authorities. If these disciplines could be shown to derive from ancient pagan wisdom and to be compatible with Christian truth, they gained legitimacy. The doctrine also served broader humanist ambitions to establish the continuity and superiority of ancient pagan learning. Yet scholars now also recognize that <i>Prisca Theologia</i> was not merely cynical rhetorical strategy: Renaissance thinkers genuinely believed that the discovery and study of ancient sources would yield profound truths about the nature of reality and the human soul.</p>

<h2>Transmission and Variant Forms</h2>

<p>The concept of a unified ancient wisdom has roots stretching back to early Christian apologetics. Church fathers like Justin Martyr and Clement of Alexandria argued that pagan philosophers had intuited truths about God that aligned with Christian revelation — a position that allowed them to incorporate Platonic thought into Christian theology. However, the specific formulation of <i>Prisca Theologia</i> as a doctrine of sequential wise men transmitting a complete and coherent philosophical system emerged distinctly in the Renaissance. Medieval Islamic scholars, particularly al-Ghazali, had similarly argued for the compatibility of Greek philosophy and Islamic revelation. Ficino's innovation was to bind this concept specifically to Hermes Trismegistus and to use the <i>Corpus Hermeticum</i> as the textual evidence for the doctrine's validity. Variants of <i>Prisca Theologia</i> appeared throughout the early modern period: the Hermetic variant emphasized Hermes and Egyptian wisdom; the Kabbalistic variant emphasized Hebrew revelation; the Pythagorean variant emphasized numerical and musical harmony as expressions of divine order. All shared the conviction that ancient wisdom, properly recovered, would yield access to cosmic truth.</p>

<h2>Related Concepts</h2>

<p><i>Prisca Theologia</i> is inextricably linked to <a href="../dictionary/magia_naturalis.html"><i>Magia Naturalis</i></a>, natural magic, which gained intellectual legitimacy through the doctrine that if the ancient sages had practiced magic, then Renaissance scholars could do the same. The concept also stands in essential relationship to <a href="../dictionary/philosophia_perennis.html"><i>Philosophia Perennis</i></a>, the perennial philosophy, which represents the later, more self-consciously constructed doctrine of universal spiritual truths underlying all traditions. <i>Prisca Theologia</i> is similarly related to the <a href="../dictionary/way_of_hermes.html">Way of Hermes</a>, understood as the path of wisdom and practice handed down from antiquity. The doctrine also informs understandings of <a href="../dictionary/theurgy.html"><i>Theurgy</i></a>, since the ancient sages' command of celestial magic was seen as evidence of their access to genuine wisdom about the structure of reality.</p>

<h2>Literature</h2>

<p>Hanegraaff, Wouter J. <i>Hermetic Spirituality and the Historical Imagination: Altered States of Knowledge in the History of Religion</i>. Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 2022.</p>

<p>Walker, D. P. <i>The Ancient Theology: Studies in Christian Platonism from the Fifteenth to the Eighteenth Centuries</i>. Ithaca: Cornell University Press, 1972.</p>

<p>Yates, Frances A. <i>Giordano Bruno and the Hermetic Tradition</i>. Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1964.</p>

<p>Copenhaver, Brian P. <i>Magic in Western Culture: From Antiquity to the Enlightenment</i>. Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 2015.</p>

<p>Iamblichus. <i>On the Mysteries</i>. Translated by Emma C. Clarke, John M. Dillon, and Jackson P. Hershbell. Atlanta: Society of Biblical Literature, 2003.</p>

<p>Ficino, Marsilio. <i>Platonic Theology</i>. Translated by Michael J. B. Allen. 6 vols. Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 2001–2006.</p>

<p>Pico della Mirandola, Giovanni. <i>Oration on the Dignity of Man</i>. Translated by A. Robert Caponigri. Chicago: Regnery, 1956.</p>

<p>Agrippa, Henry Cornelius. <i>Three Books of Occult Philosophy</i>. Translated by James Freake; edited by Donald Tyson. St. Paul: Llewellyn, 2002.</p>
"""

conn = sqlite3.connect(DB_PATH)
cur = conn.cursor()

updates = [
    ("lumen_gloriae", LUMEN_GLORIAE),
    ("gnosis", GNOSIS_EXPANDED),
    ("prisca_theologia", PRISCA_THEOLOGIA_EXPANDED),
]

for slug, definition_long in updates:
    cur.execute(
        "UPDATE concepts SET definition_long = ? WHERE slug = ?",
        (definition_long, slug)
    )
    print(f"[UPDATED] {slug} ({len(definition_long)} chars)")

conn.commit()
conn.close()

print(f"\n{len(updates)} concepts updated and saved to database.")
